# Move Semantics, Perfect Forwarding, and RVO in C++

These three C++11/14/17 features are deeply interconnected and together form the backbone of efficient resource management in modern C++.

---

## 1. Move Semantics

### The Problem
Copying large objects (like `std::vector`, `std::string`, custom buffers) is expensive. Before C++11, transferring ownership always required a deep copy.

### The Solution
Move semantics let you **steal** resources from one object (the *moved-from* object) into another, leaving the source in a valid but unspecified state.

```cpp
#include <utility> // for std::move

class Buffer {
    char* data;
    size_t size;

public:
    // Constructor
    Buffer(size_t n) : size(n), data(new char[n]) {}

    // Destructor
    ~Buffer() { delete[] data; }

    // Copy constructor (deep copy)
    Buffer(const Buffer& other) : size(other.size), data(new char[other.size]) {
        std::memcpy(data, other.data, size);
    }

    // Move constructor — steals resources, O

